A||N||6||Elenor84||Where is the E-Brake Sensor?||||1||
Z||000000||Elenor84||03-20-2001||12:58 AM||||Hey guys...here's the situation...I put the ebrake down and the light pops up so I kinda do it again and again tell it goes off...well I'm just getting sick of it...do u suggest I just buy a new e-brake cable or just disconnect the sensor?  thx!<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||862M4inCA||03-20-2001||01:07 AM||||first of all is the e-brake connected and adjusted? I know when I had mine disconnected the light would stay on even with the brake fully down. The cable doesn't retract causing the BRAKE light to remain on. Now that I have it all hooked back up the light doesn't even come on until I pull the brake up to the first notch.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Elenor84||03-20-2001||01:13 AM||||So how do I ajust it?<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||862M4inCA||03-20-2001||01:25 AM||||if everything is connected properly then the way to adjust the brake is by locating the equalizer bar. This is the part where the brake cables from each caliper and the cable from the brake handle meet at the back of the car near the cradle. You will need to have a wrench (14MM I believe). You should see the adjustment nut located on the cable end coming from the brake handle (this is on the left hand side of the cradle). All you need to do is to take all the slack out of the cables by running the nut back toward the cable. I found that the cable on mine tried to turn while I was turning the nut and found a pair of vice-grips handy to help hold the cable into place. Once all the slack is taken out the cables should be adjusted enough to prevent the light from coming on.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Elenor84||03-20-2001||03:44 AM||||Alright...I will try this tomorrow...can I do this with the brake in place because I do not have jack stands only drive on stands...so the ebrake will have to be on...unless I just leave it in gear....will that work?<BR>thx!<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||03-20-2001||10:14 AM||||Yes, I think it could work.  If the brake is on, instead of tightening the slack in the cables you will be pulling the cables tighter against the return springs at each caliper.  Though having the car up makes things easier, it should be doable without the car jacked up.<P>If you can, I would reccomend doing it on level ground without jacking it up, so you can leave the ebrake off.  That way you wouldnt be working against the springs while tightening the adjustment nut in the equalizer.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Elenor84||03-20-2001||05:50 PM||||Well Iwill try to fit my head under there but its mighty low...hopefully it will all work out...thx guys!	<BR><P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
